Fold the menu into sections, and write down how to explain things

The menu listed thirty documents flat. The section titles were small grey
capitals with nothing around them, so the groups ran together and there was no
way to see where one ended. Each section is now a bordered block that folds,
showing its title, a chevron and how many documents it holds. Learn and Explore
start open because that is where a new reader begins; the long reference lists
start shut. What the reader folds is remembered in their own browser, except
that navigating to a page inside a folded section always unfolds it -- a menu
that hides the page you are reading is worse than one that ignores a preference.

CLAUDE.md gains rule 10, on how to explain. It exists because four explanations
in a row were metaphors standing in for demonstrations: "PyTorch has to find the
layers", "a node with many neighbours would shout over a quiet one", "put a
pulse on A". Each had to be replaced by output showing the thing happening --
changing P15's reading from 4 to 999 and printing the output not moving, rather
than asserting that its own value is lost.

Rule 9 already said explanation belongs in the READMEs and that a claim needs a
worked example. Rule 10 says how to build one: intuition before implementation,
prerequisites first, why and not only what, and a fixed order for anything about
a model -- the data, what it computes, the loss, what is differentiated, the
gradient, the optimiser, then the notation, then the code. It governs answers
given in conversation as much as files, because that is where most explaining
happens.

